title: units
---



```python
units(scale=None, ref=None)
```

All units (or the units of one scale) with names, parents and source keys.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name   | Type        | Description                                             | Default   |
|--------|-------------|---------------------------------------------------------|-----------|
| scale  | str \| None | A scale id (see ``geo.scales()``); default: every scale | `None`    |

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type             | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |
|--------|------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | pandas.DataFrame | Every row has ``geo_id``, ``scale``, ``name``, ``parent_id``, ``area_km2``, a ``<scale>_id`` column for each coarser scale it lies in, and the codes each source uses for it (e.g. ``ine_code``), which ``geo.link(..., key=...)`` matches. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia
import geo4bolivia as geo
import plotly.express as px

m = geo.units("municipality")              # names, parents, INE codes, areas
display(m[["geo_id", "name", "ine_code", "province_id", "department_id"]].head())
px.histogram(m, x="area_km2", log_y=True, title="Municipal areas (km²)")
```

